Trace fork and posix_spawn too, because a child nobody asked for looks like one that failed.

CIDER_TRACE_EXEC covered exec only, and exec is the LAST step. Two applications reach the point of
needing a child process and attempt no exec at all, and nothing said whether they got as far as
asking. The same switch now also prints:

  CIDER_EXEC fork=try                    every sys_fork
  CIDER_EXEC fork=failed code=N          a fork that the kernel refused
  CIDER_EXEC spawn=try path=... arg0=... every sys_posix_spawn, with its first eight arguments

Most applications spawn rather than fork and exec by hand, so spawn=try is the line that says a
child was asked for at all.

The gate is shared rather than copied: __cider_exec_trace_on is no longer static, because the
/proc/self/environ reader it depends on lives in execve.c and three copies of that would be three
chances to drift.

CONTROL, because a trace that prints nothing looks exactly like a thing that did not happen:
  cider shell /bin/sh -c '/bin/echo hi'
prints two fork=try lines alongside the exec lines for bash, path_helper, sh and echo.

WHAT IT ANSWERED IMMEDIATELY, and the two applications turn out NOT to share a cause:

  iTerm2 (#228): fork 2, spawn 0, exec 3. The two forks and three execs are the launch chain, the
  same pair a bare bash --login produces in the control. So iTerm2 asks for NO child process by any
  mechanism, and every theory about a refused fork or a broken spawn is dead.

  CMake (#235): fork 3, spawn 1, exec 3, and the spawn is named:
    CIDER_EXEC spawn=try path=/usr/bin/uname arg0=/usr/bin/uname arg1=-r
    CIDER_EXEC fork=try
  and then NOTHING. No exec for uname, no fork=failed, and /usr/bin/uname exists and is executable
  at 13664 bytes. So posix_spawn forks successfully and THE CHILD DIES BETWEEN FORK AND EXEC, in
  its own setup path. That is a different defect from iTerm2 and a much narrower one.

I had guessed in the previous iteration that the two might share a cause. They do not, and this is
what shows it.

--- a/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/process/execve.c
+++ b/darling/src/libsystem_kernel/emulation/src/xnu_syscall/bsd/impl/process/execve.c
@@ -180,7 +180,9 @@
 // Every guest exec comes through here, and until this existed a process that was never created
 // looked exactly like one that was never asked for. iTerm2 spent two sessions in that gap: it
 // renders, opens a session log, and no shell process EVER appears in the process table (#228).
-static int exec_trace_on(void)
+// NOT static: fork.c and posix_spawn.c want the same switch, and the /proc/self/environ reader it
+// needs is here. One gate, read once, rather than three copies of the reader.
+int __cider_exec_trace_on(void)
 {
 	static int cached = -1;
 	if (cached < 0)
@@ -188,6 +190,8 @@
 	return cached;
 }
 
+static int exec_trace_on(void) { return __cider_exec_trace_on(); }
+
